  "title": "Human Responses to Visually Evoked Threat: intracranial EEG during virtual-reality heights, no-heights and shark stimuli (3 patients)",
  "description": "This dataset contains intracranial EEG recordings from three patients with treatment-resistant epilepsy who experienced immersive virtual-reality stimuli: a virtual-heights scenario, a no-heights baseline, and a 360° great white shark movie. Recordings were made at UCSF with depth electrodes, grids and strips, alongside skin conductance and visual-scanning measures. The data support investigation of threat processing in regions such as the insula and orbitofrontal cortex.",
  "methods_description": "iEEG was recorded with a Tucker-Davis Technologies PZ2 or PZ5 amplifier and RZ2 acquisition system at 3051.76 Hz. Stimuli were Unity-programmed VR scenes shown in an HTC Vive headset. Raw TDT MATLAB exports were converted to BrainVision float32 format without filtering, resampling or re-referencing. Electrodes were localized by co-registering pre-operative MRI with post-operative CT.",
